    "content": "Artemis II, NASA's first crewed mission to the Moon in over 50 years, took four astronauts on a 10-day mission around the Moon and safely back, splashing down on April 10, 2026. The crew tested the Orion spacecraft's systems in deep space, set a human distance record from Earth, and paved the way for future lunar landings.Key Mission Accomplishments & Actions:Crewed Lunar Flyby: NASA astronauts Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch, and Canadian Space Agency astronaut Jeremy Hansen flew around the Moon, reaching the far side.Distance Record: The crew set a new record for the farthest distance traveled from Earth by humans, reaching 252,756 miles (406,771 km) on April 6, 2026.Historic Milestones: Victor Glover became the first person of color, Christina Koch the first woman, and Jeremy Hansen the first non-U.S. citizen to travel beyond low Earth orbit.System Testing: Astronauts performed manual flying tests, evaluated life support systems, and tested the spacecraft's communication systems and new, improved toilet system.Observations & Science: The crew observed the Moon’s surface, taking photos and notes to help determine geological composition.Safe Return: The mission concluded with a successful splashdown in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of California after a high-speed, high-temperature reentry.",
